A driver copies this raw struct across a trust boundary. Assume an ABI where `value` is at offset 8 and the struct occupies 16 bytes. What risk remains after assigning both fields?

```c
struct info {
    uint8_t  flag;
    uint64_t value;
};
struct info r;
r.flag = 1;
r.value = v;
copy_to_user(u, &r, sizeof r);
```

Consider this deliberately invalid copy in a function using stack protection. Assume a conventional ABI with `is_admin` at offset 16 and a four-byte int. Why is a canary not enough to guarantee the authorization decision is safe? The C overflow has undefined behaviour; no outcome is guaranteed.

```c
struct user {
    char name[16];
    int  is_admin;
} u;
u.is_admin = 0;
strcpy(u.name, input);
if (u.is_admin) grant();
```
